y an undemocratic thing to  do I think as a reality check on this  it's very difficult to do some of these  colorful ideas that been floated around  and of course on the remain side they're  split you've got Dominic grieve arguing  in the Sunday Times that the Norway  option is is a very poor deal and he  doesn't support that and equally I think  there's much less support than is  perhaps magnified in the media for a  second referendum that would just create  even more uncertainty review back at  square one I don't think that's what  people want when they voted leave or  remain and the the comrades poll in  January showed very clearly that by  two-to-one people want this referendum  respected and I think whether people  voted leave will remain they want now is  done they want us to move on talk about  the economy public services we won't be  able to do that until we deliver brexit  so that's what we should all focus on  I mean Liam Fox for example has said  that if the result of the referendum  isn't delivered on it will unleash a  political tsunami do you think he's  right so I didn't quite catch that could  you repeat it please Liam Fox has there  written today saying that failing to  deliver on the referendum would unleash  a political tsunami do you think that's  right I was trying to choose my language  very carefully but I think that if  there's an attempt to reverse the  referendum stop brexit altogether  I think that will cut across not only  the Democratic mandate the biggest in  history we had I think also if you look  at current public opinion and how it  shifted by two-to-one as I said people  want us to respect the referendum I  think people's understand that's  basically a fundamental principle that  the democracy that we've got and I don't  want to leave on WTO terms I'd rather  we've got a deal but I don't think we  can let that EU hold us hostage and just  stubbornly resisted reasonable changes  so I think the Prime Minister's right to  send the negotiators back to bustles I  think actually one thing we should do  now rule out extending article 50 give  us some finality I think the team in  Brussels needs to change don't think  all the great work the civil servants  have done I think to close this deal  needs to be politically led and driven  and so taken into the control of the  politicians because this is a deal that  needs to be closed now and but I think  we should give it our best shot and if  not we're leaving on the 29th of March  and we'll make the best of it and people  will then be able to move on we'll have  the certainty we'll be able to manage  the risk but also be able to grasp the  huge opportunities that breaks it and  get on to talking about other things you  see you say that you would prefer to  leave with the deal but at the same time  you've been pretty scathing about the  Prime Minister's deal you're asking for  something that the EU is extremely  unlikely to budge on which is removal of  the backstop the reality is isn't it  that by not supporting the Prime  Minister's deal you're effectively  allowing other MPs to try and soften  brexit and potentially even stop brexit  altogether  that sounds like the spin the whips  we're putting around before the vote but  the reality is the vast you know this is  the biggest defeat on any vote in  parliamentary history  so obviously the deal needs to be  changed it's going to get through and we  should go back in good faith and keep  the arm of friendship extended with our  EU partners and neighbors and I think  there is still an opportunity there  Michel Barnier Donald Tusk who said we  need to know now what the Brits need  what they want so let's set that out but  of course if they're basically saying no  that they will not accept any changes  which would resolve the fatal flaws in  the deal that was put to Parliament then  we would leave on WTO terms at least as  a transition I think as an independent  third country would actually return to  the g